/* data-writing2.js */
// 和文英訳・自由英作文 追加分（すべてこのアプリ用に作成）
(function(){
const D=window.EIGO_DATA,L=s=>s.split('\n').filter(Boolean);
const wtr=D.decks.find(d=>d.id==='wtr'),wfree=D.decks.find(d=>d.id==='wfree');if(!wtr||!wfree)return;
const TR=`いくら忙しくても、朝食は抜かない方がいい。|譲歩「いくら〜でも」→ no matter how / however|No matter how busy you are, you should not skip breakfast.;;However busy you may be, it is better not to miss breakfast.|skip breakfast「朝食を抜く」。however＋形容詞＋S V。
この10年で、私たちの暮らしは大きく変わった。|現在完了＋over the past ten years|Our lives have changed greatly over the past ten years.;;The way we live has changed a lot in the last decade.|期間を表す over the past 〜 と現在完了。
彼が何を言おうと、私の決心は変わらない。|譲歩 whatever / no matter what|Whatever he says, I will not change my mind.;;No matter what he says, my decision will remain the same.|change one's mind「考えを変える」。
人と話すときは、相手の目を見るべきだと言われる。|It is said that 〜 / People say 〜|It is said that you should look people in the eye when you talk to them.;;People say we should make eye contact when we speak with others.|look 人 in the eye。
その本を読み終えるのに1か月かかった。|It took 人 時間 to do|It took me a month to finish reading the book.;;I spent a month reading the book through.|finish doing。
外国を旅して、自分の国のことを何も知らなかったことに気づいた。|realize＋過去完了|Traveling abroad made me realize that I had known nothing about my own country.;;When I traveled abroad, I realized how little I knew about my own country.|無生物主語 make 人 do。how little I knew で「どれほど知らなかったか」。
彼女は、その知らせを聞くとすぐに家を飛び出した。|as soon as / the moment / on doing|As soon as she heard the news, she rushed out of the house.;;On hearing the news, she ran out of her house.|on doing「〜するとすぐに」。
健康のありがたさは、病気になって初めてわかる。|It is not until 〜 that …|It is not until we get sick that we realize the value of good health.;;We do not appreciate our health until we lose it.|not until の強調構文。
友だちに誘われたが、宿題があったので断った。|ask 人 to do / turn down|My friend asked me to go out, but I said no because I had homework to do.;;I turned down my friend's invitation because I had homework.|turn down「断る」。homework は不可算。
もっと早く始めていれば、こんなに慌てずにすんだのに。|仮定法過去完了|If I had started earlier, I would not have been in such a hurry.;;I wouldn't have had to rush like this if I had begun sooner.|be in a hurry「急いでいる」。
自分の意見をはっきり言うことは、日本人にとって必ずしも簡単ではない。|部分否定 not always / not necessarily|It is not always easy for Japanese people to express their opinions clearly.;;Stating one's opinion clearly is not necessarily easy for Japanese people.|部分否定。
私たちが当たり前だと思っている水道水も、世界では貴重なものだ。|take 〜 for granted / 関係代名詞|Tap water, which we take for granted, is precious in many parts of the world.;;In much of the world, the tap water we take for granted is a luxury.|take 〜 for granted。
この写真を見ると、高校時代を思い出す。|無生物主語 remind 人 of 〜|This photo reminds me of my high school days.;;Whenever I look at this photo, I remember my high school days.|remind A of B。
子どものころは、早く大人になりたいと思っていた。|used to / 過去の思い|When I was a child, I used to want to grow up quickly.;;As a child, I wished I could become an adult soon.|used to＋状態動詞 want も可。
話すことより、聞くことの方が難しい。|比較：動名詞の比較|Listening is more difficult than speaking.;;It is harder to listen than to speak.|比較する2つの形をそろえる（doing / to do）。
その映画は思っていたよりずっとおもしろかった。|比較級＋than I had expected|The movie was much more interesting than I had expected.;;The film was far better than I thought it would be.|差分スロット much / far。
何を食べるかは、どれだけ食べるかと同じくらい大切だ。|as 〜 as / what 節・how much 節|What you eat is as important as how much you eat.;;What we eat matters just as much as how much we eat.|名詞節同士の比較。
彼は年の割に若く見える。|for one's age|He looks young for his age.;;He looks younger than he really is.|for one's age「年の割に」。
雨が降りそうだから、傘を持って行きなさい。|be going to / in case|Take an umbrella with you because it looks like rain.;;It's going to rain, so take an umbrella.|look like rain「雨が降りそうだ」。
駅までの道を尋ねられたが、うまく説明できなかった。|be asked / how to get to|I was asked the way to the station, but I couldn't explain it well.;;Someone asked me how to get to the station, but I couldn't give clear directions.|ask 人 the way / give directions。
母は私に、遅くなるなら電話するように言った。|tell 人 to do|My mother told me to call her if I was going to be late.;;My mother asked me to phone her if I would be late.|時制の一致に注意。
その町は、20年前とはまるで違う場所になっていた。|what it used to be / 過去完了|The town had become completely different from what it had been twenty years before.;;The town was nothing like it had been twenty years earlier.|nothing like 〜「〜とはまるで違う」。
スマートフォンのせいで、人と直接会って話す機会が減っている。|無生物主語＋fewer chances|Because of smartphones, we have fewer chances to talk with people face to face.;;Smartphones have reduced our opportunities to meet and talk in person.|face to face / in person。
目標を立てることは大切だが、それを達成するのはもっと難しい。|比較級＋代名詞の指示|Setting goals is important, but achieving them is even more difficult.;;It is important to set goals, but it is much harder to reach them.|even / much で比較級を強調。
彼女が来られなくなったのは、急に熱が出たからだ。|The reason is that 〜 / because|The reason she couldn't come was that she suddenly got a fever.;;She was unable to come because she suddenly developed a fever.|The reason is that 〜（because は不可）。
私が言いたいのは、努力は必ずしも報われるとは限らないということだ。|What I want to say is that 〜|What I want to say is that hard work is not always rewarded.;;My point is that effort does not necessarily pay off.|部分否定。pay off「報われる」。
彼は約束の時間に1時間も遅れてきた。|差分スロット an hour late|He arrived a whole hour late for the appointment.;;He was as much as an hour late.|an hour late / as much as「〜も」。
犬を飼うには、毎日散歩させる時間が必要だ。|To do, you need 〜|To keep a dog, you need time to walk it every day.;;If you have a dog, you have to find time to take it for a walk every day.|walk a dog / take a dog for a walk。
この町には、若者が働ける場所がほとんどない。|few / 関係副詞 where|There are few places in this town where young people can work.;;This town has few jobs for young people.|few「ほとんどない」。
英語を勉強すればするほど、その難しさがわかる。|the 比較級 〜, the 比較級 …|The more I study English, the more I realize how difficult it is.;;The longer I study English, the harder it seems.|how difficult it is の語順。
旅行の計画を立てるのは、旅行そのものと同じくらい楽しい。|as 〜 as / itself|Planning a trip is as much fun as the trip itself.;;Making travel plans is just as enjoyable as traveling itself.|fun は名詞（as much fun as）。
昨日は疲れていたので、夕食も食べずに寝てしまった。|without doing|I was so tired yesterday that I went to bed without eating dinner.;;Yesterday I was too tired to eat dinner, so I went straight to bed.|so 〜 that / without doing。
彼の話し方から判断すると、関西の出身だろう。|judging from / must be|Judging from the way he speaks, he must be from the Kansai region.;;From his accent, he is probably from Kansai.|judging from 〜（独立分詞構文）。
最近の若者は本を読まないと言う人がいるが、本当にそうだろうか。|Some people say 〜, but is that really true?|Some people say that young people today don't read books, but is that really true?;;It is often said that young people no longer read, but I wonder if that is true.|I wonder if 〜「〜だろうか」。
コンビニがあれば、夜中でも買い物ができて便利だ。|With 〜 / 無生物主語 allow|Convenience stores are useful because they allow us to shop even in the middle of the night.;;With convenience stores, we can buy things even late at night.|allow 人 to do。
失敗を恐れていては、何も新しいことは始められない。|If you are afraid of 〜|If you are afraid of failing, you can never start anything new.;;Fear of failure will keep you from trying anything new.|keep 人 from doing。
私の夢は、世界中の人々と英語で話せるようになることだ。|My dream is to be able to 〜|My dream is to be able to talk with people all over the world in English.;;I hope to become able to communicate in English with people around the world.|be able to（to の後に can は置けない）。
環境問題について考えることは、未来について考えることだ。|To do is to do / Doing is doing|Thinking about environmental problems means thinking about the future.;;To think about the environment is to think about the future.|主語と補語の形をそろえる。
その店はいつも混んでいるので、予約しておいた方がいい。|had better / make a reservation|That restaurant is always crowded, so you had better make a reservation.;;The shop is always busy, so you should book in advance.|make a reservation / book。
彼がそんなことを言うなんて、信じられない。|I can't believe (that) 〜|I can't believe that he said such a thing.;;It is hard to believe he would say something like that.|such a thing / something like that。`;
const FREE=`Should students be required to learn a musical instrument at school?|学校で楽器の演奏を必修にすべきか。|80～100語|I do not think students should be required to learn a musical instrument at school. First, learning an instrument takes a lot of daily practice, and students who are not interested may simply feel stressed rather than learning to enjoy music. Second, school time is limited. If every student must learn an instrument, less time will be left for subjects that all students need, such as math and languages. Of course, music can enrich our lives, so schools should offer instrument lessons as an option for students who want them.|立場→理由①（興味のない生徒の負担）→理由②（時間の制約）→譲歩と代案
Is it a good idea for families to eat dinner together every day?|家族で毎日夕食をとるのはよいことか。|70～90語|I think it is a good idea for families to eat dinner together every day. First, dinner is often the only time when all family members are in the same place. Talking about the day helps parents notice if their children have problems at school. Second, eating together helps children learn good manners and try new foods. Although it may be difficult for busy families to do this every day, even a few shared meals a week can make a difference.|立場→理由①（会話の機会）→理由②（食習慣）→譲歩
Should Japan introduce a four-day work week?|日本は週4日勤務制を導入すべきか。|80～100語|I think Japan should introduce a four-day work week. First, Japanese people are known for working long hours, and many suffer from stress and fatigue. An extra day off would give them time to rest, spend time with their families, and pursue hobbies. Second, rested workers are often more productive. Some companies that have tried shorter weeks report that employees finish the same amount of work in less time. Of course, it may be hard for some industries, such as hospitals, to adopt this system, but it is worth trying where possible.|立場→理由①（健康・家庭）→理由②（生産性）→譲歩（業種による難しさ）
What is the most important skill for living in the 21st century?|21世紀を生きるうえで最も大切な能力は何か。|80～100語|I believe the most important skill for living in the 21st century is the ability to keep learning. Technology is changing so quickly that much of what we learn in school may be out of date in ten years. For example, many jobs that exist today, such as app developer, did not exist when my parents were students. People who can learn new skills on their own will be able to adapt to such changes. In short, learning how to learn is more valuable than any single piece of knowledge.|答え→理由（変化の速さ）→具体例→結論（言いかえ）
Should people be allowed to use smartphones while walking?|歩きながらスマートフォンを使うことを認めるべきか。|70～90語|I do not think people should use smartphones while walking. The main reason is safety. People who look at their phones while walking pay less attention to cars, bicycles, and other people, and they often cause accidents on station platforms and crowded streets. In addition, they can be a nuisance to others because they walk slowly and suddenly stop. If people need to use their phones, they can simply step aside and stop for a moment.|立場→理由①（安全）→理由②（迷惑）→代案
Do you think online friends can be as important as friends you meet in person?|オンラインの友人は、実際に会う友人と同じくらい大切になりうると思うか。|80～100語|I think online friends can be as important as friends we meet in person. First, the Internet allows us to meet people who share our interests, even if no one around us does. For example, I became close to a student in Taiwan through an online drawing community, and we encourage each other every week. Second, it is sometimes easier to talk honestly with someone who does not know our daily lives. Of course, it is important to be careful about privacy, but distance does not decide the value of a friendship.|立場→理由①（共通の関心）＋体験→理由②（本音を話しやすい）→譲歩→結論
Should schools start later in the morning?|学校の始業時刻を遅くすべきか。|70～90語|I think schools should start later in the morning. Research shows that teenagers' bodies naturally want to sleep and wake up later than adults'. When school starts early, many students are short of sleep and cannot concentrate in morning classes. If classes began at around nine instead of eight thirty, students would be more awake and learn more effectively. Although parents' working hours and bus schedules would need to be adjusted, students' health and learning should come first.|立場→根拠（体内時計）→仮定法で効果を示す→譲歩→結論
What do you think is the biggest problem in your community, and how can it be solved?|あなたの地域で最大の問題は何か。どう解決できるか。|80～100語|The biggest problem in my community is that many elderly people live alone and have few chances to talk with others. Since young people have moved to cities, some of them go for days without speaking to anyone. One solution would be to use the empty classrooms in our local elementary school as a community space. Elderly residents could meet there for lunch, and students could visit them after school to play games or learn about local history. This would help both generations and keep our community connected.|問題（現状）→解決策（具体的に）→効果（仮定法）
Is it better to have one close friend or many friends?|親友が1人いるのと、友人がたくさんいるのと、どちらがよいか。|70～90語|I think it is better to have one close friend than many friends. A close friend knows us well and can give honest advice when we face difficult decisions. With many friends, our relationships may stay on the surface, and we may not have anyone to rely on in hard times. For example, when my grandfather was in hospital, the only person I could talk to about my worries was my best friend from elementary school. One true friend can be worth more than many acquaintances.|立場→理由（本音・支え）→対比→体験→結論
Should tourists be charged extra to visit popular sightseeing spots?|人気の観光地を訪れる観光客から追加料金をとるべきか。|80～100語|I think tourists should be charged a small extra fee to visit popular sightseeing spots. In places such as Kyoto, the large number of visitors causes crowded buses, noise, and litter, which make life difficult for local residents. A small fee would reduce the number of visitors at peak times, and the money could be used to clean streets and protect historical buildings. Some people may argue that this would discourage tourism, but a fee of a few hundred yen is unlikely to stop people who really want to visit.|立場→問題点→効果（2つ）→反論と再反論
Do you agree that "time is money"?|「時は金なり」に賛成か。|70～90語|I only partly agree that time is money. It is true that time is valuable, and wasting it can mean losing chances to earn or learn. However, time is more precious than money in one important way: we can earn money again, but we can never get lost time back. Moreover, some of the most valuable ways to spend time, such as talking with family or resting, produce no money at all. Therefore, we should value time not only for what it earns but for how it lets us live.|部分的賛成→譲歩→再反論（時間は取り戻せない）→追加の根拠→結論
Should elementary school students use tablets in class?|小学生は授業でタブレットを使うべきか。|80～100語|I think elementary school students should use tablets in class, but only for limited purposes. Tablets can make learning more interactive. For example, students can watch videos of animals in their natural habitats or use apps that let them practice math at their own pace. On the other hand, young children also need to develop basic skills such as writing by hand and concentrating on one task. Therefore, tablets should be used as tools to support lessons, not as a replacement for books, notebooks, and conversation with teachers.|条件つき賛成→利点＋例→On the other hand（懸念）→結論
What is something you would like to change about yourself?|自分について変えたいことは何か。|70～90語|One thing I would like to change about myself is my tendency to put things off. I often wait until the last minute to start my homework or prepare for tests. As a result, I feel stressed and cannot do my best work. Last month, I tried breaking big tasks into small steps and doing one step each day. It was not easy at first, but I finished my report two days early. I want to keep this habit so that I can feel more relaxed and confident.|変えたいこと→その結果（問題）→取り組み（体験）→今後
Should young people be encouraged to work in rural areas?|若者に地方で働くことを勧めるべきか。|80～100語|I think young people should be encouraged to work in rural areas. First, many rural towns are losing their populations, and young workers are needed to keep local businesses, hospitals, and schools running. Second, rural areas can offer young people experiences that cities cannot, such as farming, close relationships with neighbors, and the chance to start new projects with community support. With the Internet, it is now possible to do many jobs from anywhere. If the government provided housing support, more young people would consider moving.|立場→理由①（地域の必要）→理由②（若者の利点）→補足（ネット）→提案（仮定法）
Which do you think is more important, talent or effort?|才能と努力では、どちらが大切だと思うか。|70～90語|I think effort is more important than talent. Talent may help someone start well, but without effort, it rarely leads to great results. For example, a classmate of mine was not the fastest runner at first, but she practiced every morning for three years and eventually won the city marathon. In addition, effort is something everyone can control, while talent is not. Believing in effort encourages people to keep trying instead of giving up when they are not naturally good at something.|立場→理由①（才能だけでは不十分）＋例→理由②（自分で制御できる）→結論
Should people stop using cash?|人々は現金の使用をやめるべきか。|80～100語|I do not think people should stop using cash completely. Cashless payment is certainly convenient: it is fast, and it allows us to keep track of our spending easily. However, not everyone can use it. Some elderly people are not comfortable with smartphones, and in a natural disaster, electronic payment systems may stop working when the power goes out. In such situations, cash is the only reliable way to buy food and water. Therefore, while cashless payment should be promoted, cash should remain available as a backup.|立場→譲歩（利点）→However（弱者・災害）→結論（併用）`;
const tr=L(TR).map((l,i)=>{const [ja,hint,ms,pt]=l.split('|');return {k:'wtr2:'+i,kind:'tr',ja,hint,models:ms.split(';;'),point:pt}});
const free=L(FREE).map((l,i)=>{const [en,ja,words,model,plan]=l.split('|');return {k:'wfr2:'+i,kind:'free',en,ja,words,models:[model],point:plan}});
wtr.items.push(...tr);wfree.items.push(...free);
})();

